**Read:** [Tratamento de Minérios: Práticas Laboratoriais — Sampaio, França, and Braga](../sources/CETEM-Praticas-Laboratoriais.lingua#r_44AAWXB1D7MBR9B0V3TYB8GF9B), the full sampling/preparation and relevant process-test methods; [Tratamento de Minérios — CETEM, sixth edition](../sources/Tratamento-de-Minerios-CETEM.lingua#r_04ZQFADZ3NC3T6QDPQ565W1ERA), the complete sampling and characterization chapters; [A specimen, a sampling target, and a result about a population](../../../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH), the full shared sampling/uncertainty account.

Processing testwork depends on representative feed, preparation, stream collection, method, operating conditions, sampling support, and analysis. A convenient grab or selected mineral specimen can answer an observation question without representing the plant population or variability needed for design.

Moving streams, batches, stockpiles, particles, slurry, and changing circuits require different sampling accounts. Increment selection, mass, particle distribution, segregation, timing, recycle, and inventory affect what a composite represents. Repeated analysis of one pulp does not assess stream/time variability.

Test design should distinguish repeatability, feed variation, process settings, preparation, and measurement. Several tests from one domain or conditioning procedure are not necessarily independent evidence for a full resource or operating range. Preserve unfavorable/failed runs and changed procedures.

**Work:** complete the relevant sampling/lab chapters and design a bounded test programme for a stated process question. Trace each result to its feed/domain, preparation, operation, sampling, and analysis. Compare a plausible alternative population or changing-feed account and identify the additional test needed before scale-up.

Read complete methods and the geometallurgical/measurement foundations. Later circuit and accounting studies use this observation chain to judge whether a calculated recovery or closed balance represents the intended plant behavior.

**Continue:** [Explain crushing through material breakage, size distribution, and circuit constraints](Crushing-breakage-and-circuit-duty.lingua#r_1VA4J52FKX3H9Z80CRZM73SZ33).

